  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bellville began as a railway stop on the Cape Town–Stellenbosch line and was renamed after surveyor‑general Charles Bell in 1861.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellville%2C_South_Africa"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s original name was “12 Mile Post” because it sits 12 miles from Cape Town’s center, a halfway rest spot for oxen and wagons.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellville%2C_South_Africa"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bellville hosts the University of the Western Cape, founded there in 1960, making it a key university town in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Bellville"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first factory in Bellville was the Van Riebeeck Mineral Water Company, which appeared in the 1880s and marked the start of light industry.",
